<section id="engine">
  <h2>Chromium renders the web. Electron brings it to your desktop.</h2><p>
    Blanc is a desktop browser built with Electron, which bundles Chromium for
    rendering websites. Blanc supplies its own interface, tab management,
    blocking and permission controls. Electron lets one application support
    macOS, Windows and Linux. <a
      href="https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/process-model"
      >Electron’s process model</a
    > explains how the runtime and rendering engine fit together.
  </p><p>
    Chromium is the open-source browser engine used by several browsers. Blanc
    is not the Google Chrome application: it has its own features and update
    channel. Using Chromium does not, by itself, describe every connection a
    browser makes. Those connections are explained below.
  </p><p>
    Blanc uses official Electron releases. Engine changes arrive through a new
    Blanc release; the running app cannot independently swap in a newer
    Chromium.
  </p>
</section>
<section id="updates">
  <h2>An app update includes the browser engine.</h2><p>
    Installed release builds check Blanc’s official GitHub Releases channel. An
    update includes the application and its bundled Electron/Chromium runtime.
    Applying it requires restarting Blanc; a new engine is not downloaded into
    the running browser separately.
  </p><p>
    On Linux, Blanc is distributed as an AppImage. Make the downloaded file
    executable and run it. Its updater replaces the application image when the
    environment permits; if automatic updating fails, download the new AppImage
    from the official channel. A writable location and a working AppImage
    environment are required. AppImage packaging does not keep Chromium updated
    independently.
  </p><p>
    macOS releases are signed and notarized. Windows installers carry
    timestamped Authenticode signatures. Linux AppImages are authenticated
    through the signed checksum manifest; they do not have the same OS-level
    signing model. <a href="/trust#security"
      >Read the verification instructions</a
    > and <a href="/download">choose your download</a>.
  </p>
</section>
<section id="blocking">
  <h2>Blocking is built into Blanc.</h2><p>
    Blanc Blocker applies network rules and cosmetic filters from bundled,
    hash-verified EasyList and EasyPrivacy snapshots. It does not fetch changing
    filter lists or remote scriptlet resources at startup. Reviewed list updates
    arrive with a new Blanc release.
  </p><p>
    The blocker is independent of an extension store. You can turn it off for a
    site when something breaks, or change its setting in the app. Lists target
    known ads and trackers; blocking cannot guarantee that every tracker is
    stopped. <a href="/trust#ad-blocking">Explore Blanc Blocker</a>.
  </p>
</section>
<section id="connections">
  <h2>What can leave your device?</h2><p>
    This is an explanation of shipped behavior, not a live network monitor.
    Optional features have separate controls; ordinary website traffic is still
    part of browsing.
  </p><dl class="connections-list">
    {
      connections.map(([name, explanation]) => (
        <div>
          <>
            <dt>{name}</dt>
            <dd>{explanation}</dd>
          </>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl><p>
    <strong>This website has separate choices.</strong> Cloudflare counts page views
    without cookies, and the download redirect counts aggregate downloads. Google
    analytics and limited ad-conversion measurement load only after you choose Allow
    through the footer’s Privacy choices, or if you previously saved that grant.
    Declining or withdrawing prevents future optional measurement.
  </p>
</section>
<section id="controls">
  <h2>Choose what Blanc may send.</h2><p>
    Search suggestions and usage measurement are presented on in fresh-install
    setup. Neither sends until your choices are saved, and you can turn either
    off before continuing. Later, search suggestions live in Settings → General;
    usage measurement lives in Settings → Privacy &amp; Security. Your choices
    stay device-local.
  </p><p>
    Sync, 1Password and tab handoff require separate actions. Optional website
    measurement is controlled through this site’s footer. The <a href="/privacy"
      >privacy policy</a
    > lists data fields, recipients and retention in more detail.
  </p>
</section>
<section id="limits">
  <h2>Understand the trade-offs before choosing.</h2><p>
    Blanc has no general extension runtime or built-in AI assistant. Private
    tabs stay out of Blanc’s history and session restore; they do not make you
    anonymous to websites or your network. Blocking cannot guarantee that every
    tracker is stopped.
  </p><p>
    Memory use depends on the workload, engine version and platform. Published
    measurements are specific runs, not a promise for every computer. <a
      href="/support#electron">Read the benchmark qualifications</a
    >.
  </p><p>
    Blanc-owned code is MIT licensed. Names and logos remain reserved, and
    bundled third-party material keeps its own terms. Read the <a
      href="https://github.com/bnfy/blanc/blob/v1.25.0/LICENSE">license</a
    >, <a href="https://github.com/bnfy/blanc/blob/v1.25.0/ASSET-LICENSE.md"
      >identity carve-outs</a
    >, and <a
      href="https://github.com/bnfy/blanc/blob/v1.25.0/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md"
      >third-party notices</a
    >.
  </p><p class="works-evidence">
    App explanations checked against public <a
      href="https://github.com/bnfy/blanc/releases/tag/v1.25.0">Blanc v1.25.0</a
    >. Read its <a href="https://github.com/bnfy/blanc/tree/v1.25.0">source</a> and
    <a
      href="https://github.com/bnfy/blanc/blob/5b8fa03bda00cbd7fbcafba16384e63c7e85ff9a/docs/release-incidents/2026-10-01-v1.25.0.md"
      >release evidence</a
    >. Signed manifests authenticate downloads; they do not establish
    reproducible builds. <a href="/support">More questions?</a>
  </p>
</section>
